<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jiangsu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jiangsu is a coastal province of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and Huai rivers, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>. Its southern part, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>, was for centuries one of the richest regions of China, a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, salt and scholarship. Nanjing served as capital for several southern dynasties, the early Ming and the Republic of China.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jiangsu.jpg" alt="Jiangsu" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nanjing_CBD_from_City_Wall.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jiangsu is a coastal province of East China, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> to the north, Anhui to the west, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> to the south. It has a coastline of over 1,000 kilometres along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-sea">Yellow Sea</a>. The land is flat: plains cover 68 percent of the province, and most of it stands no more than 50 metres above sea level. The well-developed irrigation system earned the south the name shuixiang, land of water, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a> has so many canals that it has been called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> of the East. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> crosses the province from north to south, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> flows through its southern part.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the earliest Chinese dynasties the area lay far from the centre of Chinese civilisation and was home to the Huai people. Under the Zhou the state of Wu appeared in southern Jiangsu. Near the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spring-and-autumn-period">Spring and Autumn period</a> it became a great power under King Helu and in 484 BCE defeated Qi, but in 473 BCE it was conquered by the state of Yue. Yue was in turn subjugated by Chu in 333 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 221 BCE. Since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang dynasties Jiangsu has been a national economic and commercial centre, partly because of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>.</p><p>The name combines the first elements of two cities, Jiangning, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a>. In April 1927 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> established a government at Nanjing, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> separated from Jiangsu in 1927. Nanjing fell to the Japanese on 13 December 1937, and the atrocities of the following three months became known as the Rape of Nanjing. It then became the seat of the collaborationist government of East China under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-jingwei">Wang Jingwei</a>, and most of Jiangsu stayed under Japanese occupation until 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jiangsu is a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> and a population of about 85 million, the most densely populated of the country's provinces. It is divided into thirteen prefecture-level cities, among them Nanjing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a>, Wuxi, Changzhou and Zhenjiang. Most residents are ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a>, with Hui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> minorities. The southern part, centred on the Shanghai-Nanjing corridor, is the more urban and prosperous, while the north is more rural.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jiangsu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A 2025 autosomal DNA study found the ancient Liangzhu samples intermediate between today’s Han Chinese of <b>Jiangsu</b> and Zhejiang, Kra–Dai speakers and Austronesian speakers of Taiwan and the Philippines, and closer to Neolithic samples from Fujian than to modern Austronesian speakers.</blockquote><a href="/culture/liangzhu-culture">Liangzhu culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1996 and 1999 a team led by Satoshi Yamaguchi of Japan’s National Museum of Nature and Science also found many similarities between Yayoi remains from Yamaguchi and Fukuoka prefectures and remains from <b>Jiangsu</b> province on the Chinese coast.</blockquote><a href="/culture/yayoi-culture">Yayoi culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The route passes through the provinces of Zhejiang, <b>Jiangsu</b>, Shandong and Hebei and the municipalities of Tianjin and Beijing.</blockquote><a href="/work/grand-canal-of-china">Grand Canal of China · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Zhang Shicheng, a salt boatman from Taizhou in <b>Jiangsu</b>, led salt workers in revolt against Yuan Dynasty officials in 1353 and in 1354 proclaimed himself King of Great Zhou at Gaoyou.</blockquote><a href="/state/dazhou-zhang-shicheng">Dazhou (Zhang Shicheng)</a></li><li><blockquote>The regime nominally governed occupied parts of <b>Jiangsu</b>, Zhejiang and Anhui and the Chinese districts of Shanghai, but its ministers had little authority and its revenues depended on Japanese approval.</blockquote><a href="/state/reformed-government-of-the-republic-of-china">Reformed Government of the Republic of China</a></li><li><blockquote>Xu was ruled by a lineage of the Ying clan among the Huaiyi peoples of northern <b>Jiangsu</b> and Anhui, with its centre near modern Sihong.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-xu-huaiyi">State of Xu (Huaiyi)</a></li></ul></section>
